A||||11||86SE||Anit-Sway/Roll Bars - Worth it?||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||06-24-2000||05:09 PM||||My 86SE V-6 has the factory bushings and shocks.  The shocks still seem to be in excellent shape after 87,000 miles (passes the test).  If I make 80MPH 90° turns, the front end dips more than you would expect and the rear tilts like a mo fo (ok ok, about 30MPH turns....)<P>We got a $h!t load of poly, we can make poly bushings for the front sway bar.  Does the poly make a big difference in handling?<P>Then I'll buy a rear sway bar and make some poly bushings for it.  Is this all worth it??||||reg||5
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||06-24-2000||05:11 PM||||My two cents "Y-E-S" ||||reg||14
Z||000002||Ed||06-24-2000||06:55 PM||||Absolutely||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||06-24-2000||08:05 PM||||When you go to make the outer bushings,be sure and make them ball,or cone shaped where they touch the swaybar,and conrol arm.Most urethane replacements just copied the rubber design,and make cars ride like dump trucks by binding up the suspension's movements over the smallest bumps.<BR>Swaybars will make a car actually ride better if done properly.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||06-25-2000||12:25 PM||||And poly squeeks so bad that I finally removed it. All of it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-25-2000||01:51 PM||||If you don't plan on racing and you want to save some cash, try using a front sway bar on the rear.  That's what I plan on doing to the Grape Ape.  I think I'll use poly although I'll probably <B>think</B> I'm getting more out of it (the poly bushings) than I really do.||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||06-25-2000||02:20 PM||||I'm going to use a front sway bar on the rear, and fab some cylindrical poly bushings for the outer ends, so it rides and squeaks like a dump truck!  I need to haul some gravel this weekend...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000007||Monkeyman||06-25-2000||09:36 PM||||I'm sorry but I don't get the joke.  Are you against poly?  Other than the squeaking thing, is there anything wrong with poly bushings for the sway bars?  (Serious question)||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||06-25-2000||11:32 PM||||Sorry, I was just being silly.  You know, like calling Doritos performance chips - pure corn...<P>Anyway, I don't have any personal experience with poly, so I can't comment.  She seems like a nice girl, though.<P>Enough!  I knew I shouldn't have gotten into this!!  ||||reg||9
Z||000009||SCCA FIERO||06-25-2000||11:39 PM||||I have full poly bushings in the front and rear swaybars on my 88. The front is factory and the rear is a 1" Addco. The saddle bushings do squeak, but only over speedbumps when both tires rise and fall the same. The endlinks don't squeak. You can get the poly endlinks (Addco) from Autozone for just about $15 front or rear. I went to replace my front ones and they actually broke into several parts. Might be a good idea to replace them if they are original.<p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 06-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||gixxer||06-26-2000||12:36 AM||||Be sure to keep polyurethane bushes well lubed with silicone GREASE. For the sway bars this'd just be the center pivots where the bar actually turns. The endlinks  don't need any grease. Also, if the center bushes are fitted around the bar too tightly, they will burn the grease off with the friction and squeek (loudly!). I have lube fittings on my Hondas rear sway bar and give it a squirt of silicone grease about every 6 months. I use "Sil*Glyde".   ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||06-26-2000||11:55 AM||||I gotta get a new Autozone.  The local one here said they can't get the poly end links and can't get any of the center things.||||reg||1
